---
layout: null
permalink: /search.json
sitemap: false
---
{%- comment -%}
  Client-side search index, built at compile time. No plugins required, so it
  works on stock GitHub Pages.

  Only pages carrying a `title` are indexed; generated files (sitemap.xml,
  robots.txt, feed.xml, this file) have none and drop out automatically.
  Set `search: false` in a page's front matter to exclude it explicitly.

  `body` is truncated so the whole index stays well under ~200 KB — enough
  for relevance ranking, and the browser only downloads it once, lazily.

  `markdownify` before `strip_html` is load-bearing, not decorative. Jekyll
  renders pages in an arbitrary order, so `p.content` here is the RENDERED
  html for pages processed before this file and the RAW markdown for pages
  processed after it. Without markdownify the raw ones land in the index
  complete with asterisk emphasis markers and un-interpolated Liquid tags.
  Running it through markdownify normalises both cases to html, and
  strip_html then leaves clean prose either way.

  (Do not write a literal Liquid output tag in this comment. Liquid still
  lexes the body of a comment block, so it would emit a syntax warning on
  every build.)
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign indexed = site.pages | where_exp: "p", "p.title" -%}
{%- assign indexed = indexed | where_exp: "p", "p.search != false" -%}
[
{%- for p in indexed -%}
{"t":{{ p.title | jsonify }},
"u":{{ p.url | remove: "index.html" | jsonify }},
"d":{{ p.description | default: "" | jsonify }},
"b":{{ p.content | markdownify | strip_html | normalize_whitespace | truncate: 2400, "" | jsonify }}}
{%- unless forloop.last -%},{%- endunless -%}
{%- endfor -%}
]
